\par\par \b \uc2  ASIAN EXOTICS - Regionals off a bit as dollar rise. \b0\par\par\uc2 516 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 April 1998\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  0500 GMT - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  slightly off their morning highs as the dollar extends its gains against the yen, but trading remains slow. \par \par \uc2  * The rupiah hovers around the 8,000 level but looks set for further weakness amid mounting social tension in Indonesia, where student protests demanding President Suharto's resignation are assuming a more menacing tone. \par \par \uc2  At least two students were injured at the weekend in clashes with police on the island of Sumatra. \par \par \uc2  * Indonesian rating agency PT Pefindo said problem loans may account for 60 percent of the banking industry's total loans by the end of this year if the country's private debt issue is not resolved. \par \par \uc2  It cut the ratings for seven Indonesian firms and lowered the outlook on all rated local banks, citing continued deterioration in asset quality and liquidity. \par \par \uc2  * Dealers say the rupiah's fall is currently being cushioned by dollar offers near 8,100 and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's high interest rate policy. \par \par \uc2  * They say Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the money market this morning to drain excess liquidity and keep \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deposit rates high. \par \par \uc2  * Elsewhere, Malaysia's continuing water shortage and fears the Rashid Hussain group's acquisition of troubled Sime Bank may dilute its earnings are casting a pall on Malaysian markets. \par \par \uc2  Trade Minister Rafidah Aziz said in weekend news reports that she has received letters from industrialists saying they may have to stop operating if the water problems persist. \par \par \uc2  * Kuala Lumpur shares are down nearly two percent but the ringgit appears to be holding up fairly well and dealers say 3.79 will be a tough barrier for the dollar. \par \par \uc2  * The Singapore dollar is being undermined by the yen's weakness and looks set to fall through 1.60 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  * The Thai baht could continue to see some inflows from last week's Bangkok Bank share issue, but dollar demand from Hong Kong-based U.S. investment houses should cap its gains near 38.70 for the time being. \par \par \uc2  * Some dealers estimate that about three-quarters of the $1 billion raised by the share sale have already been through the market. \par \par \uc2  * The Philippine peso is weak but off its early lows, fuelled by banks buying dollars to cover forward contracts with multinational companies. \par \par \uc2  * Manila shares reached a seven-week closing low on worries about the presidential election in May and disappointing first quarter results. \par \par \uc2  * In South Korea, the finance ministry says direct foreign investment fell sharply to $572 million in the first quarter from $2.12 billion a year ago. \par \par \uc2  * This follows weekend news that the monthly jobless rate hit a 16-year high in March, with seasonally unadjusted unemployment soaring to 6.5 percent from 3.4 percent last March. \par \par \uc2  * But the won is up from its lows as traders bet on it climbing further on month-end exporter deals. \par \par \uc2  * The Taiwan dollar is back below the key T$33 level on the back of a sagging yen, but fears of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will limit its decline. \par \par \uc2  (C) Reuters Limited 1998. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du4r06rr3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
