\par\par \b \uc2  Goldman Sachs Sees Stable Quarter For Most Southeast Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 April 1998\par 14:43\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  HONG KONG -(Dow Jones)- Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  look set for a more stable second quarter, though for most, solid appreciation will have to wait until investors grow more confident in the battered region, Goldman Sachs said in a recent regional economic report. \par \par \uc2  The sole exception among traded Association of Southeast Asian Nation \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the Thai baht, which Goldman Sachs sees moving to 37.0 baht per dollar over the quarter and to 35.0 baht over the next six-to-12 months. \par \par \uc2  The strengthening will come as the country records a larger current-account surplus and more capital inflow, which will offset a deeper-than-expected downturn in Thailand's real economy this year, Goldman Sachs said in its latest Asian Economic Quarterly publication. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia's rupiah will stabilize between 7,000 and 7,500 to the U.S. dollar in the second quarter, due to the country's economic bailout and reform agreements with the International Monetary Fund, Goldman Sachs said. \par \par \uc2  Goldman Sachs indicated that the key risk to its Indonesia forecasts would be a slowdown or backsliding by the government on its economic reform and stabilization commitments to the IMF. \par \par \uc2  "Given the amount of depreciation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the state of the banking system, the external debt and the entrenched vested interests in the cabinet, the risks are high," Goldman Sachs said. \par \par \uc2  Without IMF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's economy would likely contract in both 1998 and 1999, Goldman Sachs said. As it is, Goldman Sachs is forecasting a 10% contraction of real gross domestic product and inflation of 50%. \par \par \uc2  The investment bank's figures for Thailand are only slightly better, with the economy seen contracting by 6.0% in 1998 and inflation running at about 13%. \par \par \uc2  In terms of Indonesia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stability, confidence will be an investor focus. Though the IMF agreement should stabilize the rupiah, "further appreciation is unlikely without...a clearer political picture and...settlement of the private sector's external claims," Goldman Sachs said. \par \par \uc2  Nominal interest rates are seen rising over the next three months, reflecting inflationary pressure, Goldman Sachs said. \par \par \uc2  Malaysia, whose economic growth is expected to slow to 2.5% in 1998, can expect to see the ringgit strengthen as regional sentiment improves, Goldman Sachs said. But as long as the country's top two leaders are differing on the treatment of bank shareholders during economic restructuring, capital inflows will be depressed and the ringgit's appreciation will be delayed, Goldman Sachs said. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar moved generally in line with the rupiah and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first quarter of the year, "as it has been buffeted by the contagion effects throughout the period," Goldman Sachs said. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore hasn't altered its monetary policy since reversing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's outright appreciation. To maintain price stability, the MAS continues to track the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against a trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket. That has resulted in a raising of interest rates in times of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  instability. The three-month rate, however, has eased from a peak of 12% to between 5.0% and 6.0% toward the end of the first quarter. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's growth this year is expected to hit 2.5% to 4.5%, according to government figures, and consumer prices should be only a touch higher. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1998 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020020329du4l00o57\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
