\par\par \b \uc2  Chronology of Indonesia's financial crisis. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,539 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Februar 1998\par 11:59\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Feb 20 (Reuters) - The following is a chronology of the financial crisis that has rocked Indonesia and the political developments in the country in the last eight months. \par \par \uc2  1997 \par \par \uc2  July 8 -  The rupiah starts to crumble. Jakarta widens its rupiah trading band to 12 from eight percent. \par \par \uc2  July 24 - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  meltdown. The rupiah, baht, ringgit and peso all slump as confidence in the region rapidly deteriorates. \par \par \uc2  August 14 - Jakarta abolishes system of a managed exchange rate. The rupiah sinks further. \par \par \uc2  October 6 - Rupiah hits a low of 3,845. \par \par \uc2  October 8 - Indonesia says it will ask the International Monetary Fund (IMF) for financial assistance. \par \par \uc2  October 31 - Indonesia's IMF package unveiled. It provides for as much as $40 billion in aid, although front-line defence is $23 billion. \par \par \uc2  November 5 - IMF approves a $10-billion loan for Indonesia as part of the massive international package. \par \par \uc2  1998 \par \par \uc2  January 6 - Indonesia unveils its 1998/99 budget projecting an increase of 32.1 percent growth in revenue and expenditure over the current budget and a four-percent economic growth rate. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah loses half its value against the dollar over five days, breaking through the 10,000 to the dollar level, before a slight recovery. \par \par \uc2  The freefall is triggered by perceptions that the budget was not tough enough to meet IMF-mandated austerity measures and by market talk that Jakarta might declare a debt moratorium. \par \par \uc2  January 10 - Indonesia's President Suharto comes under increasing international pressure to adhere to IMF conditions. He announces the delay or review of 15 big infrastructure projects. \par \par \uc2  January 11 - The IMF's first Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer arrives in Jakarta for talks about the IMF's programme and how to move ahead with it. \par \par \uc2  January 14 - IMF Managing Director Michel Camdessus heads for Jakarta, saying the IMF was asking Indonesia to sign a new letter of intent committing itself to implementing further IMF reforms. \par \par \uc2  January 15 - Camdessus and President Suharto sign an agreement strengthening economic reforms. \par \par \uc2  January 16 - Camdessus says he expects the Indonesian and Thai economies to recover within two years. \par \par \uc2  January 21 - The rupiah falls to a record low of 12,000 to the dollar as reports surface that corporates were now paying their dollar debts off in rupiah. \par \par \uc2  January 22 - The rupiah collapses a week after the IMF package shows no signs of alleviating Indonesia's debt and confidence crisis. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits a record low of 17,000 amid economic pessimism and on hints that Research and Technology Minister Jusuf Habibie might be the preferred vice-presidential candidate. Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pulls the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  back up to 11,800. \par \par \uc2  January 23 - Indonesia presents a revised budget sticking to figures agreed with the IMF. The budget expects zero growth in fiscal 1998 and inflation of 20 percent. But it also assumes the rupiah will be at a level of 5,000 to the dollar. It ends the day at 12,000. \par \par \uc2  January 27 - Indonesia announces a temporary freeze on debt servicing but says it is not a moratorium as companies with the dollars can pay their creditors. \par \par \uc2  It also announces banking reforms in which the government will guarantee the security of both depositors and creditors. \par \par \uc2  January 28 - Indonesia again stresses it has not sought a debt moratorium, saying a temporary freeze suggested on servicing corporate debts is entirely voluntary. \par \par \uc2  Mobs attack shops owned by ethnic Chinese in Central Java over rising food prices. \par \par \uc2  February 3 - Singapore proposes a multilateral system of guarantees for Indonesian letters of credit. \par \par \uc2  February 4 - Indonesian government gives a lukewarm response to a proposal by U.S. economist Steve Hanke to set up a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board, where the rupiah is pegged to another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , most likely the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Suharto refuses to name a running mate in March election, saying it was the right of parliamentary groups, not his right, to name the candidate. \par \par \uc2  February 6 - Thousands of troops and police put on a show of strength in practice sessions in Jakarta before the indirect presidential elections in early March. Chief debt negotiator Radius Prawiro says the country's total foreign debt was $137.424 billion. \par \par \uc2  February 9 - Growing unhappiness over Suharto's handling of the economic crisis leads to a signature campaign to replace him. \par \par \uc2  Suharto says he will announce new steps on the exchange rate of the rupiah, giving the first hint that the fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system might be implemented. A curfew is imposed in an eastern Indonesian town after more riots. \par \par \uc2  February 10 - Bank Indonesia Governor Soedradjad Djiwandono tells parliament the government was weighing the merits of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. \par \par \uc2  Outgoing army chief Feisal Tanjung says recent riots were inflamed by people were trying to undermine national unity. Planning Minister Ginandjar Kartasasmita declines to be vice-president candidate, paving the way for Research and Technology Minister Jusuf Habibie. \par \par \uc2  February 11 - Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad tells parliament the government was preparing the groundwork for a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. The rupiah rises to 7,200 to the dollar on Indonesia's plan to introduce a fixed-rate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Suharto also accuses unnamed parties of seeking to undermine the economy by driving the rupiah down. \par \par \uc2  Police detain 140 people after protests in Jakarta over rising food prices. \par \par \uc2  February 12 - Suharto repeats warning of a plot to destabilise the government as hundreds riot in a West Java town. \par \par \uc2  Suharto also appoints a loyalist, General Wiranto, as new military chief. The ruling Golkar party names Habibie and parliamentary speaker Harmoko as its vice presidential candidates. \par \par \uc2  February 12 - The IMF insists Indonesia is not ready for a rigid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. \par \par \uc2  February 13 - Suharto tells President Clinton the IMF was failing to stem his country's financial crisis. Clinton objects to Jakarta's plans to fix the rupiah to the U.S. dollar through a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah surges to as high as 8,100 on signs the plan will proceed. \par \par \uc2  February 14 - The IMF threatens to cut off the bail-out fund for Indonesia over its plans to peg the rupiah. ASEAN central bankers discuss a proposal to promote the use of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  for intra-regional trade. \par \par \uc2  Police detain 154 people in riots over rising prices which left at least three dead and Jakarta bans mass rallies during voting in March. Rioters had torched shops owned by ethnic Chinese, churches and cars in three west Java towns. \par \par \uc2  Two rioters are shot dead in Praya, a town in the holiday island of Lombok. \par \par \uc2  February 16 - Parliamentary speaker Harmoko withdraws from vice-presidential race in favour of Habibie, who was also endorsed by the Moslem-backed United Development Party. \par \par \uc2  As rioting over rising food prices worsens, Indonesian police warn they would shoot rioters on sight even as new military commander Wiranto played down the impact of the violence. \par \par \uc2  February 17 - President Suharto fires \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Soedradjad Djiwandono for opposing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board proposal. \par \par \uc2  Djiwandono replaced by U.S.-trained economist Sjahril Sabirin. The sacking draws immediate attacks from global lenders as it is seen as a direct affront to their demands that Jakarta reform its economy. \par \par \uc2  February 18 - Habibie assured of the vice presidency after winning the backing of the powerful armed forces. \par \par \uc2  German Finance Minister Theo Waigel says President Suharto promised to rethink the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board plan, throwing the markets into confusion about Jakarta's position on the scheme. \par \par \uc2  Waigel also signs agreements to provide Indonesia with 375 million marks ($205.3 million) in financial aid to fight the economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Thousands of students and labourers, angered by rising prices, stone dozens of shops and offices in the southeast Sulawesi capital of Kendari, while a series of hoax bomb threats are received in Ujung Pandang in South Sulawesi. \par \par \uc2  February 19 - The country's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis remains unresolved amid confusion over Jakarta's position on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board as rioting is reported in eastern Indonesia and West Java targeted at Chinese shopowners. The rupiah steady at 8,750/8,850. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Commerce Secretary William Daley and German Finance Minister Waigel insist Indonesia must follow IMF-prescribed economic reforms to get economy on track. \par \par \uc2  Chinese shopowners open a public relations drive in  Java selling commodities at discounts to Javanese poor. China says it believed Indonesia could keep ethnic unity and deal with its economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Singapore says it would make US$2 billion available if its plan for international guarantees for Indonesian letters of credit is implemented. \par \par \uc2  The IMF and United States, while stressing that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board scheme was premature, show flexibility by saying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  plan could be considered once banking reform, corporate debt and other economic issues had been addressed by Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  February 20 - Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad says Indonesia so far faced no problem in meeting state foreign debts, while telling parliament that the government wants to maintain good ties with the IMF. \par \par \uc2  Mar'ie says Suharto had instructed the finance ministry and the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prepare for the introduction of a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board by taking into consideration all views and development, locally and abroad. \par \par \uc2  Police brace for further unrest in Sulawesi island as no clear picture is seen on how the government would stabilise the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010926du2k01hqa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
