\par\par \b \uc2  Economist/ \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Boards/Asia-3: Assails IMF Crisis Mgmt \b0\par\par\uc2 340 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 1998\par 23:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1998, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Mexico in 1994 is a prime example of these events, as is Thailand, Indonesia and other countries with pegged exchange rates in Asia, according to Hanke. \par \par \uc2  Central banks 'have broad latitude to create base money,' Hanke notes, while '\b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  boards do not have this flexibility.' \par \par \uc2  'A \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board system required that domestic notes and coins, as well as deposit liabilities, be fully covered by foreign reserves denominated in foreign anchor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and that the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  must trade, without restrictions, at an absolutely fixed exchange rate with the anchor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' he said. \par \par \uc2  He noted that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  boards 'run on automatic pilot' so that changes in the monetary base are 'determined solely by changes in the demand for domestic base money,' or what he defines as the balance of payments. \par \par \uc2  Hanke has long been critical of the IMF, partly because it played a role, in his view, in the demise of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board arrangements that had been prevalent into the post-World War II period when the international monetary system shifted more toward \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  arrangements. \par \par \uc2  The IMF was created in the post-World War II period as a policeman of the global current account. And by Hanke's reckoning, it 'lent its weight and money to the establishment of new central banks' to create 'new clients and jobs for the boys,' he told the House Banking Committee. \par \par \uc2  Hanke is equally critical of the IMF in its handling of the Asia crisis. \par \par \uc2  'What the IMF has failed to realize is the fact that all of the countries in Asia where it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 1997 - Thailand, Philippines, \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  and South Korea - had the same type of fatally flawed exchange-rate systems,' he stated. \par \par \uc2  'Although the dramatic events in Asia have generated a torrent of commentary about exchange rates, most of that commentary has either been half-baked or flat out wrong,' Hanke insists. \par \par \uc2  -By William Murray, 202-862-9272, e-mail: bmurray@ap.org \par \par \uc2  (END) Dow Jones Newswires 03-02-98 \par \par \uc2  2254GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010916du2300x7w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
