\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Indonesian rupiah sinks, debt crisis mounts. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Dean Yates \par \uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1998\par 12:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Jan 22 (Reuters) - Indonesia's rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  melted to fresh lows on Thursday as the government grappled with a foreign debt nightmare and the prospect of mass lay-offs. \par \par \uc2  Businessmen and analysts said the economy was grinding to a halt because of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , liquidity and debt turmoil. \par \par \uc2  But they said the full impact of the crisis would not be felt until next month when many workers returned after a Moslem holiday period and found factories shut. \par \par \uc2  "At this (\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) level, if anything a company produces has even a bit of imported content, it is finished," said an executive at a textile firm. "We are talking about factories closing down and lay-offs." \par \par \uc2  The rupiah sank to 15,000 to the U.S. dollar in early thin Singapore trade -- down a massive 84 percent since it was at 2,400 last July before Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis swept the region. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah later recovered on concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was quoted at 12,000/12,500 by 0850 GMT. \par \par \uc2  But its plunge dragged the rest of Asia's financial markets into a downward spiral, trashing the immediate outlook for the once supremely confident region. \par \par \uc2  While Indonesia's capital Jakarta was calm on Thursday, a sense of despondency gripped residents as they watched their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crumble in the country's worst economic crisis in decades. \par \par \uc2  Hotel lobbies are eerily quiet as foreign businessmen stay home, concerned about where Indonesia's plunge into economic disaster will lead. Rising food prices have already triggered looting in East Java and panic buying in Jakarta this month. \par \par \uc2  In a stark warning, officials said on Thursday at least one million people were expected to lose jobs this year. \par \par \uc2  "The present unemployment figure stands at 2.7 million as of January," Sutikno, a spokesman for the Manpower Ministry said. \par \par \uc2  "This is expected to increase this year because at least one million people will lose their jobs in the wake of the crisis," he said by telephone. \par \par \uc2  Mainly Moslem Indonesia and the IMF announced fresh reforms last week to stem blood-letting on financial markets. But the package, which superceded earlier measures agreed in return for a $43 billion bail-out, has failed to stop the haemorrhage. \par \par \uc2  Finance Minister Mar'ie Muhammad said late on Wednesday the government was working to resolve the corporate debt problem, but reiterated there would be no government bail-out. \par \par \uc2  Banking sources said the government and the International Monetary Fund were looking at ways to deal with private sector debt. \par \par \uc2  They said the talks were not formal negotiations, but both sides were trying to find some way of dealing with corporate foreign debt estimated at around $66 billion. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia has put its total foreign debt at $140 billion, and each drop in the rupiah squeezed indebted companies harder. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta stocks opened down and kept sliding on fears over the rupiah and the debt crisis. The composite index closed down 4.82 percent at 443.53 points. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said a number of factors had savaged the rupiah early on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  They said local players had difficulty meeting maturing dollar contracts and concerns lingered over the political outlook for Indonesia after hints Research and Technology Minister Jusuf Habibie may be President Suharto's preferred vice-presidential candidate in March presidential polls. \par \par \uc2  Markets have reacted with horror to the possible elevation of the profligate Habibie, which would make him potential successor to the 76-year-old Suharto. \par \par \uc2  Suharto, in power since 1965, said on Tuesday he was ready to stand for a seventh five-year term despite unprecedented calls for him to quit. \par \par \uc2  At least 200 students staged a fresh protest against Suharto on Thursday, urging the former army general to step down. \par \par \uc2  A senior government official said on Thursday \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step up \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  "The government of \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  will do its utmost to stabilise the rupiah's value against the dollar," he said. \par \par \uc2  But economists were mainly gloomy about the corporate sector, saying most listed companies were now technically bankrupt. \par \par \uc2  Tom Soulsby, head of research for ANZ Securities in Jakarta, said the economy was likely to shrink and a 10 percent contraction was not out of the question. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and businessmen said the full impact of the crisis was not visible yet because of the annual slowdown in production during the Islamic holy month of Ramadan. Ramadan ends with the Eid ul-Fitr festival on January 30-31. \par \par \uc2  "Most workers are on holiday already," said the textile executive. "I think when they return next month, many of them will find their factory gates locked." \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1m02gjb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
