\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  shudder as rupiah succumbs. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1998\par 10:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 22 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah broke down on Thursday amid mounting jitters about the country's debt crisis, a mere week after a package of stiff new IMF-led reforms sparked hopes of real change. \par \par \uc2  It dived 20 percent to 15,000 per dollar within the first hour of Asian trade, casting a shadow over other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said trading was extremely erratic, with bids from brokers at levels of 16,500 and 17,000. \par \par \uc2  In Jakarta, there were reports banks had cut their interbank lines because they did not want to trade in current market conditions, while Singapore dealers also complained of a dwindling interbank market. \par \par \uc2  "People just don't want to quote this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  any more. There's still a lot of (dollar) buying interest from U.S. funds, Jakarta centres, everywhere. And nobody has any dollars to offer," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah stood at 11,600/12,000 to the dollar at 0920 GMT after Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly, selling dollars for rupiah at the 14,000 and 13,500 levels early and then at 12,500 in late trade. \par \par \uc2  A senior government official said \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step up \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and soon draw on an existing $2.0 billion stand-by loan to defend the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the market was still overwhelmingly in favour of the dollar following the government's failure to convincingly address the country's corporate debt, estimated at $65 billion, and growing bank liquidity problems. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sudradjad Djiwandono said on Wednesday the government would soon announce guidelines to resolve liquidity and solvency problems in the private banking sector. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was also hit by political worries after hints Research and Technology Minister Jusuf Habibie, viewed negatively by the markets, may be President Suharto's preferred vice-presidential candidate in March polls. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said Indonesia urgently needed to find ways to reschedule or repackage its mountain of corporate debt. \par \par \uc2  "Everyone knows now that they're not paying up...Although they've said that they won't bail out the private sector debts, I think they'll have to do something," said Chia Woon Khien, head of Asian research at Skandinaviska Enskilda Banken. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's dizzying drop did not wreak the usual havoc in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as Indonesia's problems were increasingly being perceived as a domestic crisis, dealers and analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit hit a morning low of 4.58 to the dollar, but then recovered to 4.4900/00 at 0920 GMT, still down from its late Wednesday levels of 4.4350/650. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar recovered some of its early losses, to stand at 1.7585/1.7625 against the U.S. dollar from 1.7580/30 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht fell to 54.20/40 to the dollar onshore against 53.70/53.75 late on Wednesday. Dealers said offshore baht hit an early low of 55.30 in a knee-jerk reaction to the rupiah's drop. It was at 53.20/70 at 0920 GMT. \par \par \uc2  "The baht would likely follow the rupiah's footsteps offshore. A limited local corporate demand for dollars to pay off foreign debt was offset by new inflow of foreign funds into the Thai stock market," a foreign bank dealer in Bangkok said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso stumbled to 42.72/82 per dollar against its previous 41.90 close as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept out of the market after days of indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  deputy governor Alberto Reyes said the bank would open its one-month lending facility to banks which cut their dollar overbought positions and would buy bank treasury bill holdings at market rates to add liquidity to the system. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won fell to 1,754/60 per dollar from Wednesday's 1,723 close as the rupiah's drop fanned speculative dollar purchases. \par \par \uc2  But traders said dollar sales by exporters and demand for the won before the Lunar New Year break would cushion its fall. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar surrendered its early gains drawn from the yen's rise and strong local cash demand ahead of the holidays, as players fretted about the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The Australian dollar lost half a cent to $0.6578/85 on worries the rupiah's plunge would trigger another cycle of competitive devaluations. \par \par \uc2  The Indian rupee was at 38.725/755 to the dollar from a previous 38.75/80 close and dealers said it was likely to be rangebound as the market absorbed the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's measures to tighten liquidity. \par \par \uc2  The following table shows the drop in value of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  since the crisis began in July. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are in percentage terms and reflect the local unit's fall against the dollar, not the dollar's rise. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab    Current value    Move since July 1.\par  Indonesian Rupiah     11,500.00\tab     -78.85%\par  Thai Baht\tab \tab      54.20\tab     -52.21%\par  Korean Won\tab \tab  1,754.00\tab     -49.37%\par  Malaysian Ringgit\tab     4.51\tab     -44.12%\par  Philippine Peso\tab      42.72\tab     -37.97%\par  Taiwan dollar\tab \tab  33.71\tab     -17.67%\par  Singapore dollar\tab      1.76\tab     -18.68%\par  Hong Kong dollar\tab      7.74\tab \tab 0.06%\par  Indian rupee\tab \tab   38.73\tab      -7.53%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (c) Reuters Limited 1998\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1m02g9k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
