\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Indonesian crisis seen halving corn imports. \b0\par\par\uc2  By K.T. Arasu \par \uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Januar 1998\par 10:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Jan 22 (Reuters) - Indonesian imports of corn and soymeal, the main ingredients in animal feed, were set to plunge this year as the country's economic crisis squeezes meat and poultry consumption, industry sources said on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  They said the feedmeal sector, which has been growing by up to 15 percent a year during the last seven years on the back of rising incomes, could take as long to recover fully. \par \par \uc2  "There has been a significant fall in feedmeal production because of the country's economic crisis," Budiarto Soebijanto, chairman of the Federation of Feedmeal Producers, told Reuters. \par \par \uc2  He said soymeal imports were expected to fall by 50 percent to some 500,000 tonnes this year, while corn imports would range between zero and 500,000 tonnes, depending on local production. Indonesia imported about one million tonnes of corn last year. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia produced 10.3 million tonnes of corn in 1996, but output is estimated to have fallen by about 30 percent last year because of a drought linked to the El Nino weather pattern. \par \par \uc2  Industry sources said they expected output to increase by about 10 to 15 percent this year from the 1997 level, barring any recurrence of drought conditions in growing areas. \par \par \uc2  Indonesia is facing its worst economic crisis in decades, with the rupiah \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  having lost some 80 percent of its value against the U.S. dollar since last July. \par \par \uc2  The government has projected zero growth and an inflation rate of 20 percent in fiscal 1998/99 (April-March), while one million workers are expected to lose their jobs this year. \par \par \uc2  "I think the industry will take six to seven years to recover fully," one industry source said. "We have to rebuilt the consumer base all over again." \par \par \uc2  "Workers in the lower income group are our main customers. They eat an egg a day, and least a (chicken) leg or wing. They now need income improvement and new jobs," he added. \par \par \uc2  He said the country would need to redevelop labour-intensive sectors such as the property and textile industries, which have been slowed considerably by the economic crisis. \par \par \uc2  Industry sources said the amount of feedmeal sold in the local market was expected to have declined by 25 percent in 1997 and another 25 percent this year from 3.5 million tonnes in 1996. \par \par \uc2  "Feed production costs have increased by more than three times because of the high cost of imported raw material coupled with \b \highlight22\uc2 operational\b0 \highlight\uc2  costs," one source said. \par \par \uc2  He said the cost of imported raw materials, which constitute 80 percent of total raw material costs, have increased sharply because of the rupiah's fall against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  "Production costs have increased to 2,380 rupiah/kg from 700 rupiah last July at an exchange rate of 10,000 rupiah to the dollar and if based on July prices," he said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah fell to a fresh low of 15,000 to the dollar on Thursday but regained some lost ground after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The sources said many of the 40 feedmills in \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  had reduced production by between 30 and 40 percent, with some even cutting back output by more than 50 percent. \par \par \uc2  "Many feedmills are facing problems in opening LCs (letters of credits) while others are having cash flow problems. There is talk that some have completely halted production," one said. \par \par \uc2  He said export credit guarantees offered by the United States could be useful to some feedmills, but added that one constraint was that imports would have to be at least 30,000 tonnes. \par \par \uc2  "Under the current circumstances, 30,000 tonnes would be viewed as a giant shipment," he said. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1m02g6u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
