\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Downtrodden Asia seen hurting global markets. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Davison \par \uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1998\par 09:39\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Jan 12 (Reuters) - Asian markets were stuck in crisis on Monday despite soothing words from U.S. officials visiting Jakarta for high-level meetings that spurred hopes of a solution to economic chaos in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  Major stock markets in Hong Kong, Tokyo and Sydney were all weaker, while the yen fell against the U.S. dollar and the Thai baht resumed its slide, hitting fresh record lows. \par \par \uc2  "Obviously, with this sort of movement, the rest of the world cannot ignore what is happening in Asia," said Miles Remington at SocGen Crosby. \par \par \uc2  Rapid falls and persistent weakness were expected to drag London shares lower, with dual-listed shares in Hong Kong banks spreading the gloom that has settled over Asia. \par \par \uc2  The outlook for Wall Street was more uncertain, with some traders debating whether Friday's 2.8-percent move in the Dow Industrials had already factored in much of Monday's Asian market chaos, especially with bond yields at historic lows. \par \par \uc2  In Hong Kong, the blue chip Hang Seng index crashed through the key 8,000 barrier soon after the opening. \par \par \uc2  The index shed 11 percent before recovering to close 773 points or 8.70 percent weaker at 8,121 162, reflecting the possible liquidation of high-flying investment bank, Peregrine Investment Holdings, and rising interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo sank 2.21 percent to 14,664 sparking renewed jitters about the health of its banking sector, while Australian shares were down 2.33 percent at 2,542. \par \par \uc2  But Jakarta bucked the regional trend on market hopes that renewed talks between senior officials from the United States and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) could find a resolution to Indonesia's woes. \par \par \uc2  The outcome of talks between the IMF and President Suharto would not be made public until Thursday. \par \par \uc2  But IMF First Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer said the talks went well. "This was a very, very good meeting. We agreed on how to proceed," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta stocks were up 1.16 percent to 346.96 while the rupiah was slightly weaker at 8,600/9,000. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers was expected in Jakarta later on Monday to follow up on Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by President Bill Clinton, who stressed the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to comply with economic reforms attached to an IMF aid package valued at US$43 billion. \par \par \uc2  There was however mounting concern of an impending political crisis in Jakarta, with renewed calls for Suharto to step aside after ruling Indonesia with an iron fist for 32 years. \par \par \uc2  Two prominent Moslem leaders on Monday added their voices to weekend demands for him to step down by Megawati Sukarnoputri, daughter of the man Suharto replaced in 1965. \par \par \uc2  The mood remained bleak elsewhere in the region. \par \par \uc2  Weaker markets in Hong Kong, Tokyo and Sydney prompted renewed fears of a spread of the Asian contagion to the region's healthiest economies. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar peg to the U.S. dollar remained firm, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards widened and interest rates soared in another sign of the spread of the U.S. dollar hedging that has crushed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout the region. \par \par \uc2  Taipei fell victim to the trouble in Hong Kong, with the main stock index closing 4.68 percent weaker at 7,375.14, while the weaker yen helped to pummel the Taiwan dollar below a 10-year low at T$34.40 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Singapore stocks were also off by more than 10 percent at 1,050.88 at 0755GMT while the Singapore dollar continued to fall, reaching 1.7940/90 against 1.7690/7740 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht hit fresh record lows of 56.50 to the U.S. dollar, with players unimpressed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to curb speculation. The Malaysian ringgit was also weaker, helping to depress the main stock index by around 3 percent to 476.79. \par \par \uc2  But South Korea gained favour, with stocks up 3.50 percent at 456.20. Regional dealers said the opening of the Seoul stock market to 100 percent foreign interest had forced a change in weightings in key benchmark indices, forcing investors with long-term positions to raise their weightings. \par \par \uc2  The interest helped the Korean won to firm, although regional woes capped the upside. \par \par \uc2  Yields on South Korean three-year corporate bonds also dipped on Monday on active buying. The three-year yield sank to 22.7 percent from Saturday's close of 24.05 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1c09bh3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
