\par\par \b \uc2  Asian markets mood bleak as U.S. visits Jakarta. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sarah Davison \par \uc2 633 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Januar 1998\par 07:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1998 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  HONG KONG, Jan 12 (Reuters) - Asian markets were stuck in crisis on Monday despite soothing words from U.S. officials visiting Jakarta for high-level meetings that spurred hopes of a solution to economic chaos in Indonesia. \par \par \uc2  "It's horrendous, just horrendous," said one head trader at a major brokerage, referring to sentiment throughout the region. \par \par \uc2  Major stock markets in Hong Kong, Tokyo and Sydney were all weaker, while the yen fell against the U.S. dollar and the Thai baht hit fresh record lows. \par \par \uc2  But Jakarta bucked the regional trend, reflecting market hopes that renewed talks between senior officials from the United States and the International Monetary Fund (IMF) could find a solution to Indonesia's woes. \par \par \uc2  The outcome of talks between the IMF and President Suharto would not be made public until Thursday, officials in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  But IMF First Deputy Managing Director Stanley Fischer said the talks went well. "This was a very, very good meeting. We agreed on how to proceed," he told reporters. \par \par \uc2  Jakarta stocks were up 2.58 percent to 351.81 at the morning close while the rupiah was steady around Friday's close at 8,350/8,850. \par \par \uc2  U.S. Deputy Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers was expected in Jakarta later on Monday to follow up on Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by President Bill Clinton, who stressed the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  to comply with economic reforms attached to an IMF aid package valued at US$43 billion. \par \par \uc2  But there was mounting concern of an impending political crisis in Jakarta, with renewed calls for Suharto to step aside after ruling Indonesia with an iron fist for 32 years. \par \par \uc2  Two prominent Moslem leaders on Monday added their voices to weekend demands for him to step down by Megawati Sukarnoputri, daughter of the man Suharto replaced in 1965. \par \par \uc2  The mood remained bleak elsewhere in the region. \par \par \uc2  Major stock markets in Hong Kong, Tokyo and Sydney were weak, with an 11-percent slide in Hong Kong below the critical 8,000 barrier, prompting further fears of a spread of the Asian contagion to the region's healthiest economies. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar peg to the U.S. dollar remained firm, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forwards widened and interest rates soared in another sign of the spread of the U.S. dollar hedging that has crushed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout the region. \par \par \uc2  Taipei fell victim to the trouble in Hong Kong, with the main stock index down 4.68 percent to 7,375.14. \par \par \uc2  Singapore stocks were also off by more than 100 points or nearly nine percent to 1,071.66 at 0445 GMT while the Singapore dollar continued to ease, reaching 1.7855/05 against 1.7690/7740 late on Friday. But overnight rates fell, indicating greater calm. \par \par \uc2  Tokyo stocks were off 2.21 percent at 14,664.44 on continued speculation about government steps to boost the economy. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht hit fresh record lows of 55 to the U.S. dollar, with players unimpressed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures to curb speculation. The Malaysian ringgit was also weaker, helping to depress the main stock index by 3.43 percent to 474.74. \par \par \uc2  But South Korea gained favour, with stocks up 3.50 percent at 456.22. Regional dealers said the opening of the Seoul stock market to 100 percent foreign interest had forced a change in weightings in key benchmark indices, forcing investors with long-term positions to raise their weightings. \par \par \uc2  The interest helped the Korean won to firm, although regional woes capped the upside. \par \par \uc2  Yields on South Korean three-year corporate bonds also dipped on Monday on active buying. The three-year yield sank to 22.7 percent from Saturday's close of 24.05 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1998 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010925du1c09bda\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
