\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  idle on New Year's eve. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 829 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Dezember 1997\par 09:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 31 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders sat on their hands on Wednesday as holidays in several centres, including Tokyo, severely inhibited trade on New Year's Eve. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won remained prey to year-end dollar demand from importers and companies scrambling to meet debt payments. It ended at 1,695 to the dollar against Tuesday's 1,590 close. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said sentiment was boosted by news the International Monetary Fund (IMF) had approved payment of $2 billion to Seoul under an accelerated schedule to ease South Korea's credit crunch. \par \par \uc2  Foreign commercial and investment banks also pledged on Tuesday to roll over billions of dollars of short-term debt, giving South Korean banks an extra month's breathing space. \par \par \uc2  The won's weakness was limited to the 1,800 level against the dollar as a result of all the good news, but there was no consensus among dealers over the won's longer-term prospects. \par \par \uc2  "If more foreign lenders roll over loans to Korean companies and provide fresh loans, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market could improve quite quickly in January," said a foreign bank dealer in Seoul. \par \par \uc2  Selling orders were also placed around 1,800 won for three-month non-deliverable forwards in overseas markets. \par \par \uc2  But some dealers remained pessimistic. \par \par \uc2  "Right now things look slightly better for Korea. But there's still a lot of bad news that's not filtered through. I think the won will be in worse trouble next year," said a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar shed its early gains and slipped to T$32.638/698 to the U.S. dollar at 0800 GMT from Tuesday's T$32.600 close as exporter sales of the U.S. dollar were offset by importer demand in an otherwise slow year-end market. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sheu Yuan-dong said Asia's financial turmoil would affect Taiwan in 1998, but the government would press forward with financial reforms. \par \par \uc2  He told an annual year-end news conference the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would keep its target zone for the overnight interbank rate at 6.5-7.0 percent and would only \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the face of abnormal or speculative factors. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar edged up to 7.7472/82 against the U.S. dollar from Tuesday's 7.7493/03 close. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Indonesian rupiah remained soft, hitting lows of around 5,800 to the dollar in subdued offshore trade with Jakarta \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets closed for book squaring. \par \par \uc2  Singapore dealers said Jakarta banks were updating screen prices at lower levels than the actual traded rates in an effort to suppress the rupiah's decline. \par \par \uc2  "They're trying to window-dress the rate down. We even saw a quote of 4,800/6,000 for $10 (million). But there's nearly no market here," a European bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they had not seen any sign of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Singapore markets and the rupiah would struggle to rise above the 5,000 level after Tuesday's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to boost it. \par \par \uc2  An Indonesian court's decision to postpone the liquidation of Bank Jakarta, one of 16 banks ordered closed by the government in November as part of economic reforms, was expected to be poorly received by the market once trading resumed. \par \par \uc2  The bank is part-owned by Probosutedjo, President Suharto's half brother. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was very thinly traded as many participants were out after the government changed the New Year's market holiday to January 2 from December 31. \par \par \uc2  The baht slid to 47.60/48.20 to the dollar onshore against 47.05/15 late on Tuesday. The offshore rate was at 46.80/47.30 against 46.20/46.70. \par \par \uc2  The Bank of Thailand projected the government would post a current account deficit of $4 billion, or 2.4 percent of gross domestic product (GDP), for 1997, a government spokesman said. \par \par \uc2  Akapol Sorasuchart also said Thailand's GDP was expected to grow at 0.3 percent in 1997 and contract by 0.7 percent in 1998. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar recovered from a brief dip below the 1.68 per U.S. dollar level after Prime Minister Goh Chok Tong said the economy grew at 7.6 percent in 1997, above the government's higher-end forecast of seven percent. \par \par \uc2  Goh said in a New Year message the Singapore dollar should remain strong and imported inflation should be subdued in 1998. \par \par \uc2  But he added that next year would be more difficult for Singapore as trade would be hit by slowing growth in neigbouring countries. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit also pulled off its early lows to stand at 3.8600/700 to the dollar in very thin trade against 3.8710/910 four hours earlier. \par \par \uc2  Philippine markets were closed for a holiday. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtcv00mdp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
