\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Korean malaise stalks Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 748 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Dezember 1997\par 11:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 10 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were shrouded in gloom on Wednesday as the South Korean won sank to new lows despite government efforts to calm shaky markets. \par \par \uc2  The won hit its daily lower limit of 1,565.9 to the dollar within the first half hour of trade, down almost seven percent from Tuesday's 1,460 close. Trading was suspended for the rest of the day. \par \par \uc2  A Finance Ministry suspension of business at five merchant banks and worries about Seoul's ability to redeem short-term foreign debt obligations precipitated the won's descent. \par \par \uc2  Dealers scoffed at Finance Minister Lim Chang-yuel's package of measures to stabilise the markets and draw in foreign funds, focusing instead on the suspension of the banks after last week's closure of nine merchant banks. \par \par \uc2  "The measure is just a peanut. Foreign investors are not fools," said a foreign bank dealer in Seoul. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea sold dollars in value-today and value-tomorrow transactions in a desperate bid to prevent corporate defaults. \par \par \uc2  But there was little sign of respite for the won. \par \par \uc2  "Without dollar supplies from the outside, the upbeat pressure for the greenback will not be easily changed," said Lee Chang-sun, senior analyst at LG Economic Research Institute. \par \par \uc2  North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were worst hit by the won's woes. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar fell through the T$32 support level to the U.S. dollar, ending at T$32.229 from Tuesday's T$31.963 close. \par \par \uc2  Taipei dealers attributed the drop partly to active non-delivery forward buying of U.S. dollars by foreign fund managers following the Taiwan dollar's recent failure to extend its gains significantly. \par \par \uc2  But exporter U.S. dollar sales and fund inflows to the stock market were expected to contain the Taiwan dollar's decline. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar hit a six-week low of 7.7450/60 to the U.S. dollar and forwards rose sharply in the face of short-selling by overseas players. \par \par \uc2  "We're hearing there are a lot of European funds trying to get out of the stock markets in Korea and Hong Kong," a said U.S. bank dealer in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The blue chip Hang Seng Index ended 4.08 percent lower at 11,022.41, its low for the day. \par \par \uc2  In Southeast Asia, the Indonesian rupiah remained in the hotseat as players swooped on any mention of President Suharto's health in thin, easily-spooked year-end markets. \par \par \uc2  State Secretary Murdiono's announcement that Suharto would attend an informal summit of the nine-member Association of Southeast Asian Nations (ASEAN) in Kuala Lumpur next week gave some respite to the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  But it failed to sustain gains above the 4,400 level. \par \par \uc2  "There's still a lot of corporate dollar demand for year-end interest payments in U.S. dollars, or for hedging," a U.S. bank dealer in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah tumbled to a historic low of 4,600 to the dollar on Tuesday before Murdiono denied rumours that Suharto had been taken to hospital. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market and squeezing short-dated liquidity to prop up the rupiah, but had limited impact. \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit fluctuated in a wide range but remained largely depressed by the plight of the won and rupiah. \par \par \uc2  Talk of large dollar offers from agents of Bank Negara Malaysia above the 3.6850/6900 levels cushioned the ringgit's falls, but dealers said there was dollar buying interest around 3.6400. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar looked soft in the face of strong local and offshore bids for the U.S. dollar below the 1.62 level. \par \par \uc2  "Technically, the market is looking at 1.63. The Singapore dollar still has a long way to go compared with the others, especially the ringgit," said Ishak Ismail, market intelligence analyst at I.D.E.A. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht hovered around 42.10/25 to the dollar in thin offshore markets for most of the day. The onshore market was closed for the Constitution Day holiday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers shrugged off comments by Deputy Prime Minister Supachai Panitchpakdi that Thailand may consider lowering interest rates after the first quarter of 1998. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso finished soft at 35.24 to the dollar against a previous 35.105 after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its overnight borrowing rate to 11 percent from 12 percent. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtca01bvo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
