\par\par \b \uc2  Indonesia's Military Signals Worries Over Socio-Political Stability \b0\par\par\uc2 621 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 1997\par 13:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones Online News \par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA, Indonesia -(Dow Jones)- Indonesia's top military leader, Feisal Tanjung, said Monday that the current monetary crisis could threaten the nation's political stability, the state news agency Antara reported. \par \par \uc2  The general's comments during a hearing in parliament came as the country's battered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the rupiah, continued its tailspin, falling to an all-time low of 4,175 against the dollar amidst concerns about the health of President Suharto. Helping to drive the rupiah down was local corporate demand for dollars, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Monday's steep 3.6% plunge occurred despite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to support the rupiah. The size of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's purchases, however, was viewed by analysts as too small to cause any significant moves in the market. \par \par \uc2  Tanjung, meanwhile, told parliament that the military is "intensifying activities that will create more conducive socio-political conditions and maintain peace and order." He added that the armed forces "will continue to be vigilant against possible upheavals." The general said that measures have already been taken to foil any attempts to disturb the \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Indonesia's largest legislative body, the People's Consultative Assembly. \par \par \uc2  Analysts have raised concerns that the current monetary crisis will give way to social unrest as layoffs increase and disposable income diminishes. \par \par \uc2  The general, however, was confident that the armed forces has "done its best to maintain the nation's stability to ensure unity and cohesiveness." \par \par \uc2  The People's Consultative Assembly is scheduled to convene in March to elect the next president and vice president. \par \par \uc2  Meanwhile, in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the dollar was trading at 4,165 rupiah late in Asian dealings, up, sharply from its close Friday at 4,025 rupiah. The dollar hit its all-time high of 4,175 rupiah shortly before the end of Asian trading but was brought down by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Concerns over President Suharto's health surfaced Friday when the 76-year-old leader failed to attend a government function. Late Friday, however, the government said that President Suharto would be taking a 10-day rest to recuperate from an overseas trip. \par \par \uc2  Mounting worries about Suharto, combined with massive local demand for dollars, are expected to exert more downward pressure on the rupiah, and traders said they expect a test of 4,200 on Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that a fire at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday that killed at least 14 people did little to build confidence in the rupiah. The fire was in a new \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  building directly adjacent to \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Gov. Soedradjad Djiwandono's office. Damage caused by the blaze is expected to cause delays in clearing transactions with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Despite the slump in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Indonesian share prices closed higher Monday on trading by foreign investors aimed at year-end window dressing and in line with the rebound of some major regional markets. The Jakarta Stock Exchange index closed up 2%, or 8.835 points, to 423.613 points. Trading volume was 412 million shares with total turnover valued at 430 billion rupiah, according to a local securities house. Gainers outpaced losers by 70 to 45, with 85 shares finishing even, and 68 stocks untraded. \par \par \uc2  An analyst with a European brokerage firm said rumors that state-owned companies will spend 1% of their total capital reserves to buy shares and mutual funds to support the country's slumping market prompted investors to take buying positions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also suspected the day's gain was due to the government propping up some state-owned big-capitalization issues through state-owned brokerages. \par \par \uc2  Copyright (c) 1997 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dokument djon000020011006dtc8014c0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
