\par\par \b \uc2  Bank Indonesia Seen Buying Rupiah In Small Amounts \b0\par\par\uc2 158 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 1997\par 04:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA (AP-Dow Jones)--Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market in extremely small amounts earlier Monday to help stem the rupiah's fall, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar rose from a Friday close of 4,025 rupiah to a new all-time high of 4,082.50 rupiah before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  came into the market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The bank was seen buying rupiah both at the 4,070 and 4,065 levels, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still trading at 4,067 against the U.S. dollar at 0305 GMT (10:05 p.m. EST Sunday). \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's slide was brought about by concerns over the health of Indonesian President Suharto. The government announced last Friday that the 76-year-old leader would take a 10-day break to recuperate from a recent trip overseas. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 08-12-97 \par \par \uc2  0317GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtc809ej0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
