\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS - Rupiah, won reel to lows but ringgit buoyed. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 698 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Dezember 1997\par 11:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 8 (Reuters) - The Indonesian rupiah and South Korean won plumbed new depths on Monday but elsewhere on the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  scene the Malaysian ringgit managed a solid gain, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah's recent dire performance continued with a  vengeance and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  failed to stop the rot. \par \par \uc2  Reports that President Suharto had been advised to take a 10-day rest unsettled the market but the underlying worries about the scale of private sector debt is the main driving force. \par \par \uc2  Analysts reckon Indonesian firms have run up around $65 billion in overseas debt and about half of it remains unhedged. \par \par \uc2  This obviously bodes ill for the rupiah and is raising fears of a swathe of corporate defaults. \par \par \uc2  "It really is a bad situation and the unhedged debt is substantial," said a Singapore-based analyst who recently returned from a trip to Indonesia. "Against this background investor confidence will remain weak and it is hard to call a bottom for the rupiah." \par \par \uc2  In late business the rupiah was trading around the day's low of 4,145 per dollar, nearly 3.5 percent down on Friday's close. \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, the International Monetary Fund (IMF) loan honeymoon appeared to be over. The won dived to close at a historic low of 1,342.40 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to sell dollars at 1,340 won but traders said it was like standing in front of a train. \par \par \uc2  The unrelenting fall of the won came amid fears that more banks and finance companies face bankruptcy in the wake of the IMF's $57 billion bail-out package last week. \par \par \uc2  The collapse of South Korea's 12th largest industrial conglomerate, Halla Group, on Saturday after defaulting on loan payments triggered a rush into dollars by exporters and by importers for future payments. \par \par \uc2  South Koreans vote in presidential elections on December 18 and the impression was that some IMF conditions to overhaul the financial system could be renegotiated by the new government, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "The market is still a little nervous of that as well and generally the behaviour by exporters in Korea is to hold on to dollars as long as they can," said Kobus van der Wath, chief treasury economist with Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "The market fully realises that with the Halla Group becoming another casualty on Friday there is a lot of debt that will become more difficult to service." \par \par \uc2  The Malaysian ringgit had a brighter day with a rise to 3.6600 per dollar from 3.7180 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  It firmed in reaction to a package of economic measures put in place by Finance Minister Anwar Ibrahim last week aimed at calming the overheating economy. \par \par \uc2  "The measures were pretty well received. It was one sign the government recognised the problems," said a dealer with a Japanese bank. \par \par \uc2  A surge on the Kuala Lumpur stock market, up 14 percent at one stage in reaction to the package, helped push up the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  Thailand's baht ended steady after an initial slide, finding support from news that only two of 58 finance companies would be allowed to reopen. \par \par \uc2  The market had expected more of the firms to be allowed to resume trading and were pleasantly surprised by the government's tough stance. \par \par \uc2  The baht was at 41.850/000 per dollar onshore against 41.75/41.85 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  The offshore rate was 41.200/300 against 41.60/41.70. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar dipped in choppy activity on the won's losses and in response to Trade and Industry Minister Lee Yock Suan's comments on Sunday the government would review its three-week-old 1998 economic growth forecast of five to seven percent. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/sing was quoted at 1.6175/90 against an early 1.6215, after profit-taking on dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's dollar also slipped in sympathy with the won to T$31.900/909 per dollar compared with Saturday's T$31.775 close. \par \par \uc2  Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  desk +65-870-3840 \par \par \uc2  e-mail asia.\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 @reuters.com \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc801gon\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
