\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia \highlight\uc2  Rupiah/4,000 -2:  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Has Not Worked \b0\par\par\uc2 199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 Dezember 1997\par 06:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 1997, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  JAKARTA (AP-Dow Jones)--The Indonesian rupiah has broken the psychologically important 4,000 level against the U.S. dollar and is now trading at a new all-time low of 4,020. \par \par \uc2  Dealers say a regional slump in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values against the dollar, coupled with continued local demand for the greenback, has sparked the continued run-up in the dollar's value. It also marks the inability of Bank Indonesia, the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , to stem the tide of dollar demand through continued \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  At 0500 GMT (12:00 a.m. EST), the dollar is trading at 4,020 rupiah, up 2% from its Thursday close of 3,955 rupiah. The rupiah has now fallen 41% against the dollar so far in 1997. \par \par \uc2  'Locals still feel very uncertain about the prospects for the rupiah so they continue to sell,' said a trader at a foreign bank in Jakarta. 'Bank Indonesia can do very little to stop this bearish sentiment now.' \par \par \uc2  Bank \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier Friday selling dollars to support the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  (END) AP-DOW JONES NEWS 05-12-97 \par \par \uc2  0514GMT \par \par \uc2  CORRECTED AT 0550GMT \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020011006dtc509cog\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
