\par\par \b \uc2  FOCUS-Rupiah, ringgit catch cold after Korea deal. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jacqueline Wong \par \uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Dezember 1997\par 12:18\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 1997 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 4 (Reuters) - Joy was only fleeting for some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday after they were heartened by the International Monetary Fund's (IMF) expanded aid package of $57 billion to South Korea, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A fresh bout of selling from overseas operators drove the Malaysian ringgit and Indonesian rupiah back down the slippery slope in late trade, after they had risen in early trade. \par \par \uc2  The early mood was mildly euphoric after the aid package boosted confidence in the South Korean won, but even then market participants said they expected more blood-letting from corporate failures and downgrading. \par \par \uc2  "There was some pull back and consolidation but the bias is still against Asia," a dealer with a Japanese bank said. \par \par \uc2  He said the ringgit was sold down after Malaysian Prime Minister Mahathir Mohamad said a $3.0 billion bridge project with Indonesia would go ahead despite the shelving of other mega projects due to the sharp fall in the ringgit. \par \par \uc2  In late trade, the ringgit hit an all-time low of 3.7150/7250 per dollar compared with 3.6500/6600 mid-morning. \par \par \uc2  The assault on the ringgit had ripple effects, bruising \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vulnerable rupiah most sharply. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Indonesia\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by selectively selling the U.S. dollar from the 3,955 level to the 3,945 level. At 1040 GMT, it stood at 3,940. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they anticipated more \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action to halt further losses if the rupiah drifted to 4,000 per dollar. \par \par \uc2  A report from research house I.D.E.A. said eyes were focused on the 4,000 level with targets gapping to 4,100 thereafter. \par \par \uc2  "The idea of a joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  'shelved' till markets calmed down now removes only credible threat against a big figure move on the rupiah," it said. \par \par \uc2  Early gains in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were attributed to profit taking by those who had gone long on dollars in recent days. \par \par \uc2  The easing of South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  shortages with the IMF aid package would cap dollar gains against the won, and therefore, other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , some dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The increased dollar supply before the end of the year will help the won, but I can see the strength being short lived," a dealer with a U.S. brokerage said. \par \par \uc2  The won/dollar rate perked up to 1,130 before sliding back to 1,163 in late action against 1,196.00 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  While the inflow of billions of dollars for South Korea begins this week, loan defaults and other financial crises could limit a recovery and lead the won back to 1,300, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht continued to gain on South Korea's loan deal and its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's defence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "The baht has recovered from lows and is experiencing good activity, gaining also from profit taking in dollar longs," said Alison Seng, economist with Standard & Poors/MMS. \par \par \uc2  She said assurance from the Bank of Thailand that half the US$6 billion in foreign debt maturing in December would be rolled over could underpin the baht. \par \par \uc2  Technical support in the short term was pegged at 40.80 per dollar with traders also likely to buy dollar/baht on dips. \par \par \uc2  Dollar/baht was quoted at 41.650/850 per dollar onshore at 1045 GMT against 42.85/42.68 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar held steady despite renewed weakness in other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It came close to striking 52-month lows at 1.6160 against the dollar, but the trend was not sustained. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at 1.6100/1.6120 at 1050 GMT. \par \par \uc2  There was no impact on the Sing dollar from an announcement by de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS), that it was unlikely to take part in Seoul's aid package. But dealers said the market was mixed over whether the Sing remained a safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  or if it would decline to stay competitive with the other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso eased a notch to 35.150/300 compared with 35.25 previously, but its upside remained limited. The rise was based on a lack of demand for dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's dollar recovered sharply on the back of the won and Taipei's stronger stock market. The Taiwan dollar was quoted at T$31.866 per U.S. dollar against Wednesday's T$32.201 close. \par \par \uc2  The Hong Kong dollar was rangebound in late trade at 7.7370/7.7380 against 7.7385/7.7395 on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  (c) Reuters Limited 1997 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020011003dtc401kq5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
